  "nist-800-53": {
    fullTitle: "NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 — Security & Privacy Controls",
    pageContent: `
      <p>NIST SP 800-53 (Security and Privacy Controls for Information Systems and Organizations), currently at Revision 5, is the most comprehensive and influential security control framework in the world. Originally developed for U.S. federal agencies, its scope and rigor have made it the de facto reference for enterprise security programs globally — referenced by FedRAMP, FISMA, CMMC, HIPAA, and dozens of other frameworks that either adopt its controls directly or map to its taxonomy. Revision 5, published in 2020, expanded beyond its traditional IT security scope to address privacy, supply chain risk management, and the intersection of security and privacy as integrated disciplines rather than separate compliance domains.</p>
      <p>The catalog contains 20 control families encompassing over 1,000 individual controls and control enhancements. Organizations select a baseline — Low, Moderate, or High — based on the sensitivity of the information processed and the potential impact of compromise, then tailor the baseline through addition or removal of controls based on a formal risk assessment. The resulting System Security Plan (SSP) documents the selected controls, their implementation status, and the planned remediation of any gaps. NIST SP 800-53A (Assessment Procedures) provides the specific test procedures that assessors use to evaluate control effectiveness — creating a standardized assessment methodology that enables comparable results across different organizations and assessment teams.</p>
      <p>Aurix provides the broadest 800-53 control family coverage of any integrated platform. Lattice directly addresses the AU (Audit and Accountability), IA (Identification and Authentication), IR (Incident Response), and SI (System and Information Integrity) control families with native capabilities — log management, behavioral analytics, automated incident response, and continuous monitoring. The platform's continuous monitoring outputs can be automatically structured to populate 800-53 assessment evidence, dramatically reducing the labor-intensive evidence collection that traditionally consumes assessor and organization time during security assessments. Prism addresses the CM (Configuration Management) and SI control families' data integrity requirements while providing the automation evidence that assessors examine when evaluating control effectiveness.</p>
      <p>Sentinel addresses the increasingly prominent AI governance controls that NIST 800-53 Rev. 5 introduced — particularly the SA (System and Services Acquisition) family's requirements for trustworthy AI and the PT (Personally Identifiable Information Processing and Transparency) family's requirements for algorithmic transparency. For the RA (Risk Assessment) control family, Aurix's integrated risk intelligence — combining threat intelligence from Lattice, data risk from Prism, AI risk from Sentinel, and operational risk from the Agentic Framework — provides the cross-domain risk visibility that 800-53's risk assessment controls require. The Extensibility Network's API governance capabilities address SC-7 (Boundary Protection) and CA-3 (Information Exchange) controls for API-exposed systems.</p>
    `
  },

  "mifid-ii": {
    fullTitle: "MiFID II / MiFIR — Markets in Financial Instruments Directive",
    pageContent: `
      <p>MiFID II (Markets in Financial Instruments Directive II) and its companion regulation MiFIR govern investment services across the European Economic Area, with specific requirements for transaction reporting, best execution, algorithmic trading governance, product governance, and investor protection. For firms using AI in investment analysis, trading algorithms, or client suitability assessment, MiFID II's transparency and record-keeping requirements extend to these AI systems — creating specific governance obligations that are intensifying as AI adoption in investment management accelerates. MiFID II's transaction reporting requirements alone generate enormous data flows: investment firms must report transaction details to their National Competent Authorities (NCAs) within one business day, with 65 specific data fields per transaction — creating massive data quality obligations for the reporting infrastructure that feeds these submissions.</p>
      <p>MiFID II's algorithmic trading requirements (Articles 17 and 48) are particularly demanding for firms operating high-frequency trading systems or algorithmic order routing. Firms must have effective systems and risk controls ensuring their trading systems are resilient, have sufficient capacity, and are subject to appropriate trading thresholds and limits. Algorithm testing requirements mandate that firms test algorithms under stressed conditions before deployment. The kill switch requirements — the ability to cancel all or part of remaining orders immediately — require real-time monitoring capabilities that can detect when algorithms are operating outside defined parameters. MiFID II's best execution requirements mandate that firms monitor execution quality and report annually on their execution policies and outcomes.</p>
      <p>Aurix Prism ensures the data quality of the transaction reporting pipelines that MiFID II's reporting obligations depend on. With 65 data fields per transaction and reporting deadlines within one business day of trade execution, errors in transaction reporting pipelines create compliance breaches that generate regulatory findings and potential sanctions. Prism's real-time monitoring of transaction data pipelines detects field-level data quality failures — missing LEI codes, invalid ISIN numbers, incorrect transaction quantities — before reports are submitted to NCAs, enabling proactive correction rather than reactive resubmission. The platform's predictive quality monitoring identifies systematic data quality issues in upstream systems before they propagate into regulatory reports, addressing root causes rather than managing symptoms.</p>
      <p>Aurix Sentinel governs AI applications in MiFID II-regulated activities with the transparency and audit documentation that the directive's requirements demand. For AI-assisted investment recommendations and suitability assessments under MiFID II Article 25, Sentinel's decision capture provides the documentation of the AI system's assessment logic that clients have the right to receive upon request. For algorithmic trading systems, Sentinel's monitoring provides the supervisory evidence that Article 17 requires firms maintain of their algorithm testing and performance monitoring activities. Lattice addresses MiFID II's system resilience requirements by monitoring trading infrastructure for the availability and performance degradation that operational incidents — and their regulatory notification obligations — require immediate detection and response to.</p>
    `
  }

